<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Runtime binding policy for the DTF custom action. THIS FILE IS NOT OPTIONAL.

  SfxCA.dll — the native stub MakeSfxCA wraps around the managed assembly — has
  to pick a CLR version to host BEFORE it can load any managed code, and it has
  no way to infer it from the assembly. Its only input is a file named exactly
  "CustomAction.config" unpacked alongside the assembly. With no such file it
  falls back to the legacy default, CLR v2.0.50727, and then loading a net472
  assembly fails with:

      SFXCA: Binding to CLR version v2.0.50727
      System.BadImageFormatException: Could not load file or assembly
      'LithicBackup.CustomActions' ... This assembly is built by a runtime newer
      than the currently loaded runtime and cannot be loaded.

  That is exactly what happened here from 1.0.11 to 1.0.55: SignalLithicShutdown
  never executed a single line. The failure was invisible because the action is
  authored Return="ignore", so Windows Installer logged
  "returned actual error code 1603 but will be translated to success due to
  continue marking" and carried on to InstallValidate — which then raised the
  1611 "unable to close all requested applications" the action existed to
  prevent.

  useLegacyV2RuntimeActivationPolicy is required for v4: SfxCA activates the
  runtime through the legacy CorBindToRuntimeEx path, and without this flag that
  request for a v4 runtime is refused.

  Packed into the .CA.dll because the .csproj lists it as <Content>, which the
  WixToolset.Dtf.CustomAction targets forward to MakeSfxCA. Renaming it, or
  dropping the Content item, silently returns the action to never running.
-->
<configuration>
  <startup useLegacyV2RuntimeActivationPolicy="true">
    <supportedRuntime version="v4.0" />
  </startup>
</configuration>
